WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.750
Flexagons are a staple of recreational mathematics, but it's always the

00:00:04.299 --> 00:00:07.649
hexaflexagon that you see. One based on a hexagon. It's got six edges.

00:00:07.649 --> 00:00:09.360
And so I'd - you know

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:15.420
someone called Vihart has made a whole series of videos about these things. I'm gonna go for the forgotten

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:21.689
flexagon. The tetraflexagon, one with four edges. And to make it we're gonna use a square of paper.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:24.210
So the first step is to fold it in half

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:26.279
whichever way you want,

00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:29.500
they're both the same. And one of the reasons why I love the

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.079
tetraflexagon is you don't need any glue. If you're making any of the hexaflexagons

00:00:34.079 --> 00:00:36.419
you have to get some glue and stick bits together,

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:41.939
whereas this one is just folding and cutting. So the first half and half has split it into

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.770
four sections long ways. I'm now gonna fold it this way and

00:00:47.559 --> 00:00:53.459
if I make the creases really, like good creases, because they're gonna kind of be used as

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.180
hinges later, I should end up with;

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:05.280
there we go - it's a piece of paper now split into 16 smaller squares. I'm gonna remove the center four squares.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.049
I'm gonna keep this ring of 12 squares intact. I'm going to take these middle ones out.

00:01:10.049 --> 00:01:13.919
You could do that by stabbing the scissors in and just cutting around but as

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:18.450
we learnt from Katie Steckles in her fold and cut theorem video,

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:24.570
you can do anything you want in a single cut if you're a bit clever about how you fold it.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:27.720
So one cut down there, come on,

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.570
and we get - that's not bad.

00:01:31.479 --> 00:01:35.669
Okay, there's the center four squares gone, we can get rid of those, and

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:41.670
now we've got the twelve round the outside. So this is now going to be three easy steps and one difficult step.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:45.720
So we start on the left and we go around clockwise. First step is easy,

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.809
take the four on the outside, fold them up and over. Everything should line up.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.269
Second step is we come around, take the three at the top, fold them up and over and they come down like that.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:02:00.089
Next step as we keep coming around is take these three, fold them up and over, and that is the end of the easy steps.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:06.419
The only difficult step is when you take up the bottom ones, you want to lift these up as if we did it

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:10.020
before that first fold. Because we want this to be symmetric so there's no beginning or end.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:14.549
So what you've got to do is move the original fold out of the way without ruining it

00:02:14.799 --> 00:02:19.379
then lift this one up and make sure the bit next to it goes underneath.

00:02:19.379 --> 00:02:23.879
So we kind of take all of this bit next to it and just feed it through

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:32.459
until it's underneath, and the rest should follow, and just kind of snap into place and there you are. There is your four edged

00:02:33.099 --> 00:02:38.669
flexagon - the tetraflexagon. So what's amazing about the tetraflexagon is it's got a lot of faces.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:45.509
So we've got face one on the front here, which I'm going to number with ones and then if we fold it over. Okay

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:49.859
so ones on the front. We've got twos on the back. So face one and face two.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:52.530
So you got twos on that face,

00:02:53.319 --> 00:03:00.238
okay, you got ones on that face, and then on the other side of the ones we've got - well it's blank so we'll label that with

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:07.649
a three. So that's face three. So this is a shape with at least three faces. We got face three there. We got face

00:03:08.379 --> 00:03:10.619
one there, and I got face two there.

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:17.578
And what happens is every time you fold it closed one way - so I'm forwarding the ones closed with the twos on the outside -

00:03:17.579 --> 00:03:20.069
I can open it up on the opposite side.

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:21.549
So it closes this way

00:03:21.549 --> 00:03:27.899
but then I open it the other way around and I get a new face. And so I'm gonna label that one -

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:34.079
where have I gone so far? I've got one there. I got two there. Let's label this one four

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:42.839
and then let's see, if we fold this this way, no that way,

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:46.139
then go this way - there's another one, that's five.

00:03:46.479 --> 00:03:51.749
You're looking for six faces because if we think of the original shape we made - there's my ring of twelve.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:57.089
So there's 12 on the front. There's 12 on the back. So I've got 24 of these little squares.

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:58.889
I'm using up four for each one

00:03:58.889 --> 00:04:01.289
so if I divide that by 4 I get

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:07.290
6. So there are enough squares to make 6, whether or not it does is a slightly different question.

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:15.720
4 is there - oh, there it is, there's 6. Okay. So, I'm gonna label this one here 6.

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:22.039
And if we actually want to check that we've got all of them; face two,

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.570
fold it in - face one, fold it in - face three.

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.080
Okay, we could pull the whole thing apart. So if we pull it back out again,

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:35.029
disassemble it, there we go. Okay, so there's all the fac-

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:37.910
we've numbered every single square at some point, to make all of them.

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:41.869
There are two different ways to put this back together.

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:47.330
And if you do it the wrong way, you will get the mirror image kind of what we started with.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:53.030
The numbers won't line up again. But if I carefully put it back together the same way we folded it originally,

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:55.190
we're back to where we started. There we go.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.170
And once you make one of these - it's kind of fun. You play with it for a while. If

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:07.520
you've not made one before it takes a bit to work out where all the faces are.

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.369
Obviously, you don't have to - I've just put numbers. I'm not very creative.

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:15.679
If you were one of these doodling mathematicians you would draw pictures or you know be artistic.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.580
I've just numbered it which is a bit sad. I'm sorry. 
- (Brady: It's Numberphile!)
- It is Numberphile,

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.710
it's not doodlephile is it so...

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:26.600
I've numbered it. So you could be as creative as you want and you can spend a while trying to find them,

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:32.360
but if you are into numbers, after a while you're like, hang on. I know there are only six faces, but

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:35.929
what combinations of faces can I get at the same time?

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:43.070
Obviously I can get one opposite two, first one I started with. I can get three opposite one,

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:45.529
can I get three opposite four?

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:51.170
Can I get five opposite - like, what combinations of faces can you see at the same time?

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:53.299
And that was the first thing I started wondering

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:58.909
when I played with one of these. And for a while, I thought I - you can get

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.580
combinations of faces, but there's always a one and a two.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:04.339
You never lose those original two faces. But no,

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:10.400
here's a five opposite of four. So you can - you can get reasonably lost right? The original two faces are long gone.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:16.970
We have two new faces on the outside and I, when I heard it I was like I want to work out the logic behind

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:23.029
this. What is it, which means I can get different pairs? And which ones are possible? And how do I go between them?

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:25.030
And so I drew a diagram.

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:33.829
Okay, diagram. We're gonna use colours, because that's how we roll here. This is as close to artistic as we get so

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:38.810
what face is definitely possible? So I first did this - actually I didn't do colours. What I did

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:41.869
I was kind of lazy. I was like, okay, I'm gonna label that as being one,

00:06:42.479 --> 00:06:45.559
Two. So it's not a fraction 1/2. It's a one opposite of two,

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:50.000
I was using my own notation. And then I went okay, I can go from that to one, three.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:52.789
So I put 1/3 on my diagram. And then I realised, I was like wait a minute.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:57.889
One face always stays there. So whenever you flex, whichever one goes on the outside

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:01.759
is still there when you open it up. You only change one at a time.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:06.710
So when you go from 1/3 to 1/2, the 1 doesn't change. I was like, well I can kind of imagine

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:10.519
those ones being linked. And so I can go from 1/3 to 1/2.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:15.620
If I keep the 3 I can get to 3/6, so over here somewhere.

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:18.739
I've got 6 and 3 and I've kind of linked those up like that,

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:22.160
right, and I started drawing the network to try and work it out. I'm gonna show you the network

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:24.559
I ended up with because I'm particularly pleased with it.

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:31.130
First of all, I'm gonna put this back to its starting state. One's gonna be red. So this - there's one and one can be opposite...

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:37.549
let's do light blue. It can be light two. There we go, there's two. So one is opposite two.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:44.059
Okay, so, let's see. What else can I get opposite one? Well, I can go from one - that can be opposite three.

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:46.369
Okay, so I can have one three so I'm gonna put one

00:07:46.889 --> 00:07:49.879
three down here - that can be purple, or

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:53.510
one can be opposite - It's actually. it's horizontal and vertical.

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:54.599
So if I fold it

00:07:54.599 --> 00:08:00.049
vertical fold you get three, if I fold a horizontal fold you get the other one. So it's five in there.

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:04.639
So five, you're gonna be an orangey colour, and we'll put five, uh,

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:06.660
here.

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:11.089
And so what I've done is I have run all the

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:15.980
ones in a line down here. So this is all the ones you get, which have a one in them.

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:18.469
And so I've still kind of got them on either side so you can still see what's going on.

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:22.249
Okay, we'll do two next. So two, if we fold it

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:26.660
horizontally, it's opposite six. So that's two opposite six that way

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:29.119
so that's going to be six with two. And

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:34.400
then if I hadn't folded it horizontal, if I fold it vertically two is opposite four.

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:38.720
So four is gonna be dark blue and so that's gonna be down here.

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:44.660
So now I have lined up all the faces with two and actually we've got another line going this way.

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:51.139
So there are five combinations we found first, and how you can go between them, but there are other ones. So if I go

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:56.749
forward two - let's go back over here. There's one, two in the middle again. Let's go out to one of these.

00:08:56.750 --> 00:09:03.410
Let's go out to one opposite five. So we're over here. If I keep five there I can get five

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:07.850
opposite four! Okay four's over here. So what I can actually do is

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:10.640
extend the five line

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:12.600
down here

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:15.920
and then I can extend the full line up here,

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:20.990
and then I've got five, four. And that predicts I can pop down here to four, two. Because I've, like,

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:26.240
preemptively locked that in. But if I keep four on the outside close it - sure enough. There

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:33.319
it is. Four, two. So that's dropped me down there. And I predict, on the opposite side over here if we extend

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:39.860
six down there, and we extend three up there, that predicts we can have a six, three over there. Sure enough

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:42.150
let's get over that side,

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:43.410
as

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:47.600
where should we go? So we're at four, two. Let's go back to two, one.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:50.930
Oh we're back at two, one. Done that. Let's go,

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:54.650
let's go down. We'll go to one, three. Which way's that? We don't know.

00:09:54.900 --> 00:10:01.249
There's one opposite three. And then we should be able to pop up there to be three opposite 6, sure enough, there it is.

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:03.110
There's three and six, right? And then that's it.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:04.260
These other ones,

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:09.499
they don't fill in. I was able to create - it's kind of a graph, but I've put some structure into it.

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:11.150
I've had straight lines.

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:17.209
So I've got 6 different coloured lines, and whenever two of those lines crossed that combination of faces is possible.

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:19.519
So originally I just kind of sketched them all over the place,

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:26.449
and then I realised there was some logic underneath them, and I was able to then arrange it to show the structure behind

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:32.659
what combinations of faces are possible. And that pleased me immensely.

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:41.239
So I've been throwing some names of flexagons around. The way it works is you've always got the number of

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:47.389
faces, followed by the number of edges, followed by flexagon. So this has got four edges.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:55.310
So this is a tetraflexagon. And because it's got six faces it's a hexa-tetra-flexagon. Six faces, four edges,

00:10:56.010 --> 00:11:00.900
flexagon. So the ones you see online all the time are hexa-flexagons;

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:02.720
because they're made from hexagons,

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:06.700
they've got six edges. And then however many faces. The easy one, the one you see all the time is the

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:14.600
tri-hexa-flexagon, it's only got three faces. This one gives you six, out of the box. With a lot of work you can make a

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:20.060
hexa-hexa-flexagon. So six edges, six faces. My goodness, it's a real pain.

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:23.659
So, the tri-hexa takes a bit of work, making a chain of

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:26.899
nine equilateral triangles? Technically ten 'cause you'll want to glue.

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:28.280
Once you've got those ten

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:32.540
you've got to fold them, and glue them, and you only get three faces. If you make a chain of 19

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:37.069
equilateral triangles, you've got to fold it. You've got to fold it again. You've got to glue it, right.

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:43.500
You've got to realise you did that wrong, pull it apart. In my experience and a lot of effort later you get six faces.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:44.500
Whereas this - no glue!

00:11:44.510 --> 00:11:49.910
No messing around. Cut out a square. Cut out a smaller square. Bit of folding.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:53.930
Six faces are all yours. And the middle bit - guess what you can do with this!

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:55.800
I think we all know what we're gonna do with this.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:12:01.189
You're gonna make yourself a *smaller* tetraflexagon. And the bit you cut out of that - you're gonna make a smaller one again, right?

00:12:01.190 --> 00:12:05.240
You're gonna make fractal - you're gonna make gradually smaller and smaller. An infinite

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:07.440
series of

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:13.310
diminishing - they do approach a limit of total amount of paper. Hexatetraflexagons, like, ah! This, the forgotten

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:15.710
flexagon is best flexagon.

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:22.849
I've sat at a conference, albeit a recreational math conference, at the conference dinner. And the person next to me has gone

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:26.150
Do you wanna see some new flexagons? And I've gone yes!

